      "rev_n_7_0dbbc6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_0dbbc6_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_0dbbc6",
        "thought_text": "Consider the parity of N. If N is even, Alice can always ensure she gets at least half of the total sum by taking the highest available card each time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2950223823653694768",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501247.0866766
      },
      "rev_n_8_85a87a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_85a87a_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_85a87a",
        "thought_text": "If N is odd, Alice can aim to get slightly more than half of the total sum by strategically choosing cards that maximize her advantage while minimizing Bob's.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2950223823653694768",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501247.086692
      },
      "rev_n_9_c447f4_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_c447f4_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_c447f4",
        "thought_text": "Use a greedy algorithm where Alice always takes the largest remaining card, and Bob does the same. This approach ensures they both play optimally based on the current state of the game.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2950223823653694768",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501247.0867
      },
      "rev_n_10_4381ba_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_4381ba_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_4381ba",
        "thought_text": "If N is even, both players can achieve the same total score by taking turns picking the highest available card.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8735642426145156972",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501255.1355038
      },
      "rev_n_11_bb3692_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_bb3692_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_bb3692",
        "thought_text": "For odd N, Alice can always ensure she gets at least half of the total score plus one point by starting with the highest card and then mirroring Bob's moves.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8735642426145156972",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501255.1355224
      },
      "rev_n_12_b89357_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_b89357_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_b89357",
        "thought_text": "A dynamic programming approach could be used to calculate the maximum score difference for any given state of the game, but it might be overcomplicated for such a simple problem.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8735642426145156972",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501255.1355298
      },
      "rev_n_13_83fef7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_83fef7_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_83fef7",
        "thought_text": "Consider the sum of all cards. If the total sum is even, Alice can always mirror Bob's moves to ensure they end up with equal scores. If the total sum is odd, Alice can take the highest card initially to make the remaining sum even, then mirror Bob's moves.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9143699009413999840",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501275.1031666
      },
      "rev_n_14_a14d81_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_a14d81_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_a14d81",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to calculate the maximum score difference Alice can achieve over Bob. Create a table where dp[i][j] represents the maximum score difference Alice can get over Bob when considering the first i cards and j turns left.",
